<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles de Thierry</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1793–1864</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Charles Philippe Hippolyte de Thierry</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles de Thierry was an adventurer of French émigré family who claimed to have bought land at Hokianga in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> in 1820 and in 1835 announced his intention to make himself sovereign chief of the country. His claim alarmed the British Resident <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-busby">James Busby</a>, who responded by gathering northern chiefs to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-the-independence-of-new-zealand">Declaration of the Independence of New Zealand</a>. De Thierry arrived in 1837, but his settlement failed and he later lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-de-thierry.jpg" alt="Charles de Thierry" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographer unidentified · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_de_Thierry,_after_1853.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
